\subsection{Scenario projections}\label{sec:r-homes}

Capacity overstates near-term housing by two orders of magnitude (Table~\ref{tab:homes}).
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Build-out.} In four cities, zoning and plan build-out exceed the homes added in the test window
@@bo_lo@@- to @@bo_hi@@-fold. Part of the gap is definitional: build-out is an end state, not a two-year flow.
\item \textbf{Likelihood weighting} brings totals to @@lk_lo@@--@@lk_hi@@ times the observed.
\item \textbf{Trend.} A five-year trend ranks districts better in @@of_trend@@ cities: Spearman
@@trend_rho_lo@@--@@trend_rho_hi@@, against @@lk_rho_lo@@--@@lk_rho_hi@@.
\item \textbf{Manhattan rezonings.} Capacity projected @@g_capacity@@ homes on development sites, or @@g_prorated@@
pro-rated to 2025; the likelihood-weighted projection gave @@g_likely@@, against @@g_observed@@ built
(Table~\ref{tab:replay}, Figure~\ref{fig:replay}). The observed count lies in the model's 95\% range in @@of_g@@
rezonings.
\end{itemize}

