\subsection{Decision rules}\label{sec:r-dec}

In the simulated scenario for @@vs_d@@ Manhattan districts:
\begin{itemize}
\item @@vs_rules_phrase@@, and a Condorcet winner exists in @@vs_condorcet_winner_exists@@;
\item the residents' referendum picks the council's plan @@vs_resid_phrase@@, and the council's winner
@@vs_nash_phrase@@;
\item one insincere ballot could change the Schulze result in @@vs_schulze_manipulable@@ districts;
\item the winner survives a median @@vs_robust@@ of joint perturbations of coefficients and seats, and @@vs_weights@@
of redrawn stakeholder weights.
\end{itemize}
These describe the simulated scenario, not public opinion. They make explicit what a hearing leaves implicit:
resident and environmental interests are inputs to the decision, and a recommendation should be reported with its
rule and with how it changes under others.

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{What a planner gets.} For any proposed change, PlanArena shows:
\begin{itemize}
\item its effects on @@n_indicators@@ indicators, with ranges;
\item whether zoning and the plan allow it, and how likely it is;
\item what the optimizer would recommend instead;
\item how the choice depends on the decision rule.
\end{itemize}
Each output has a stated test on observed data (Table~\ref{tab:design}), and untested indicators are labelled. For one
system, this closes the gap between claimed and demonstrated contributions of PSS and urban twins
\citep{vonk2005,azadi2025,kruse2026}.

\paragraph{Design guidance.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Use each layer for the question it answers.} Zoning and plans add little to predicting \emph{where}
development happens once existing conditions and context are known. They help predict \emph{what} it becomes and
bound \emph{how much} a site can hold. This is consistent with rezoning following opportunity \citep{gabbe2018},
upzoning's varying short-run effects \citep{freemark2020,dong2024,greenaway2023} and partial plan implementation
\citep{laurian2004,berke2006}.
\item \textbf{Report likelihood-weighted projections beside capacity,} and a trend beside both. Capacity overstates
near-term housing by orders of magnitude, and @@trend_guidance@@.
\item \textbf{Constrain optimization by plausibility and show the cost.} Realism layers change recommendations and
lower the objective.
\item \textbf{Report decisions with their rule.} @@dec_guidance@@
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Limitations.}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Impacts} are validated only in Manhattan; in the other nine cities they are unvalidated estimates from
national or regional coefficients.
\item \textbf{Untested indicators.} Job change by block, and access, mix, green cover and runoff after a change, have
no observed test.
\item \textbf{The cross-city test} uses short windows (one or two years) and city-specific outcome definitions. In
Dallas, plan and zoning are undated current layers, so its gains are upper bounds.
\item \textbf{Assumptions.} The optimizer is heuristic, and the votes rest on assumed utilities.
\item \textbf{Not studied.} Usability with planners is not evaluated, and causal effects of zoning or plans are not
estimated.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

Land-use and location decisions have multidimensional, spatially interacting and uncertain effects, yet they are
decided at hearings where those effects are summarised in a few indicators and residents do not vote. PlanArena, a
land-use optimizer, measures the effects of any parcel-level change on social, economic and environmental
indicators, with what is permitted, planned, likely and recommended, and turns stakeholder interests into votes. It runs on official data for ten large US cities
at interactive speed, and every component is tested against what happened. The tests also shape the design:
\begin{itemize}
\item regulatory layers inform what development becomes and how much it could hold, not where it happens;
\item likelihood-weighted projections are far closer to what was built than capacity, while trends rank districts
better;
\item recommendations change when they must be plausible, and with the rule used to choose.
\end{itemize}
Code, data manifest and results are open, so the system and its tests extend to other cities.

\paragraph{Data and code availability.} The code, the result files behind every number, the figures and a manifest
of every input (URL, retrieval date, SHA-256) are in the PlanArena repository. \texttt{make reproduce} rebuilds data
and results from public sources, and \texttt{planarena/tools/screenshots.py} regenerates the Studio figures. Each
city's ingestion report documents coverage, crosswalks and estimated fields. Owner and mailing fields in assessor
records were never requested or read. No human participants were involved.

\paragraph{Declaration of AI assistance.} [To be confirmed by the author.] The author used Claude (Anthropic) to
write and test code, run analyses and draft text. The author designed the study, reviewed code and results, read the
cited sources and takes responsibility for the content. Every reference was checked against the record its DOI or
ISBN resolves to.

